{
  "text": "The first year of this project focused on how underground water moves in areas prone to slope failures. After reviewing existing studies, several field locations were selected where water‑movement tests could be carried out from a known source. Quick‑release water pulses and draw‑down tests were conducted in drilled holes to estimate the soil’s ability to let water flow. Additional investigations monitored changes in the water table near an injection point and examined how the size of the drill holes affected the test outcomes. Experiments using colored and fluorescent dyes were started, but the dyes were only found up to about two meters away, leaving uncertainty whether the limited detection was due to very slow flow or the dyes sticking to the soil. Plans are now underway to repeat the tracer work with a radioactive hydrogen isotope."
